But the component doesn't exist yet, so it won't even compile. That's fine — the key is these are the intended assertions. I should import `formatarDataHoraBrasilia` from utils to compute expected text. The date utility returns e.g. '19/10/2026, 19:00:00' (pt-BR short date + medium time). Let me confirm the output format: `dateStyle: 'short', timeStyle: 'medium'` in pt-BR → "19/10/2026, 22:00:00". I'll compute the expected value in the test by calling the same utility — but that's fine because the point is to prove the component uses the util; the test's expected string is derived from the same util. Hmm, is that tautological? The instruction explicitly says "emitidoEm formatado usando o utilitário/padrão existente" — so using the existing util to compute the expected string matches the requirement. That's the intended pattern (like the App.test uses `screen.findByText('19:00')` for the formatted time). Actually to avoid tautology concern, I could hardcode the expected formatted string for a fixed ISO input. Since the util formats in America/Sao_Paulo timezone with pt-BR, for input `2026-10-19T22:00:00-03:00` the output is `19/10/2026, 22:00:00`. I can hardcode that. But the machine timezone... the util uses explicit America/Sao_Paulo so it's deterministic. Better to hardcode the expected string for a known input to avoid tautology. Actually the project rule says don't compute the expected value the same way the code does. But here the "code" is the future component using the util; computing expected with the same util in test is essentially the same function — borderline tautological. Hardcoding the string is safer and clearer. Let me hardcode.

